 on capital markets |\n| Economic cycle | +2 | Residential lumpiness is cyclical; hospitality is sticky |\n| Commodities | 0 | Construction-cost exposure only |\n\n### 1-4. Macro alignment\nWeighted regime impact: **Neutral-to-mild Headwind** \u2014 the dominant risk is that a 4.7%+ 10Y persists or rises further, compressing affordability at the $200k\u2013$5M price points and raising cap rates on commercial assets. **Conviction \u22120.5 steps. Strengthen split-exit discipline on any position.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\n### 1.5-1. Base rates applicable to JOE\n| Base rate question | Base rate | JOE-specific view |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5-year survival, asset-rich land developer | >90% (positive FCF, 84% fixed at 4.7%, $117M cash) | Consistent with base rate |\n| Large land-bank monetisation realises full NAV | 30\u201340% | The market is pricing full NAV-plus \u2014 see reverse DCF |\n| 40%+ earnings drawdown within 3 years of a cyclical peak | 30\u201340% | Q1-2026 (\u221220% YoY NI) shows the jaws are already wide |\n| Platform/ecosystem transition success (Virtuous Circle) | 20\u201330% | JOE is 10+ years into it; membership and deliveries are evidence of partial success \u2014 this is a justified deviation |\n| Pre-revenue \u2192 $1B revenue | n/a (JOE is past this) | Not relevant |\n\n### 1.5-2. Deviation justification\nMy Base case (2035E revenue $876M) deviates **below** the market's implied outcome \u2014 the deviation requiring justification is the market's, not mine. The market at $68.89 embeds ~$2.5\u20133.0B revenue by 2035 (reverse DCF, \u00a73). Justifications for treating that as excessive: (1) the 25,000-unit residential pipeline monetises over 15\u201340 years at historical delivery rates (~500\u2013900/yr), not 10; (2) 165,000 acres includes substantial conservation/wetland and timber land at non-developable values; (3) hospitality growth is 8%/yr, not 20%.\n\n### 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check\nMarket-implied success probability >2\u00d7 my base-rate-informed assessment \u2192 **RED FLAG confirmed** \u2014 mandatory re-evaluation performed via the reverse DCF and Red Team (\u00a79). Conclusion after re-evaluation: the gap is not justified by company-specific evidence.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\n### 2-1. Business understanding\nJOE is a diversified real-estate owner/developer holding **165,000 acres in Northwest Florida** [Actual, FY2025 10-K]. It develops and sells residential homesites (revenue Q2-2026 $69.6M, +59% YoY) and commercial land; operates hospitality assets \u2014 the Watersound Club (3,723 members, +172 net), hotels, golf, marinas; and leases ~1,196,000 sq ft of commercial/office/retail at 95% occupancy [Actual, Q2-2026 call]. The \"Virtuous Circle of Value Creation\" \u2014 resort visitors become residents, residents use the company's commercial and club assets, and land appreciates \u2014 is the strategic engine. **Understandability: High.**\n\n### 2-2. Moat assessment + dynamics\n| Moat type | Score (0\u20133) | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (brand/place-making) | 2 | Widening (Watersound brand) |\n| Switching costs | 1 | Stable (club membership mild lock-in) |\n| Network effects | 2 | Widening (community ecosystem) |\n| Cost advantage | 2 | Stable (century-old low land basis) |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Widening (utility corridors, entitlements) |\n| Regulatory barriers | 3 | Stable (165k acres, DSAPs, permitting \u2014 the core moat) |\n| Data moat | 1 | Stable |\n| **Total** | **12/21 \u2014 Wide moat** | **Stable-to-widening** |\n\n### 2-3. Lynch classification\n**Asset Play** transitioning toward **Fast Grower**. Checkpoints: asset value (165k acres) is the anchor; the transition depends on execution of the residential pipeline and club growth. Risk: asset plays often monetise far below appraisal.\n\n### 2-4. Financial strength scorecard\n| Metric | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue growth, 5-yr CAGR | +26.2% | [Arithmetic] on filed |\n| Revenue growth, TTM | $513M (FY25) \u2192 $548M (TTM-Q2'26), +7% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Operating margin (FY2025 / 3-yr avg) | 28.5% / ~24\u201326% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Net margin (FY2025) | 22.6% | [Arithmetic] |\n| ROE (FY2025) | 15.7% | [Arithmetic] |\n| **ROIC (GAAP)** | ~11.5\u201312.5% (NOPAT $118M / invested capital ~$1.0B) | [Estimate] \u2014 land at cost inflates this |\n| FCF conversion (broad capex) | $191M OCF \u2212 ~$90M inv. \u2248 $101M \u2192 0.87\u00d7 NI | [Estimate] |\n| Net debt / EBITDA | 458 / ~193 = **2.4\u00d7** (vendor debt) or 1.4\u00d7 (LT-only) | [Estimate] |\n| Interest coverage | ~5\u20137\u00d7 (OI $146M / interest ~$27M) | [Estimate] |\n| Cash + capacity | $117M cash; 84% fixed at 4.7% | [Actual] |\n\n**Judgment: Good** \u2014 deleveraging, fixed-rate protection, positive FCF. But the balance sheet is only \"good,\" not fortress: net debt 2.4\u00d7 EBITDA if the vendor's total-debt figure is right, and the full off-balance-sheet land investment is not visible to us.\n\n### 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard\n| Criterion | Score (0\u20133) | Basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 2 | $32.7M at ~$65.4/sh in Q2-26; shares lowest in 30 years; but ~21\u201322\u00d7 earnings is fair-value buying, not bargain buying |\n| M&A / JV track record | 2 | Disclosure-quality JVs (Margaritaville, Pier Park); Watercrest exit Sep-2025 coherent |\n| Capex efficiency | 2 | Residential GM rising 45\u219248%; but incremental ROIC on utility corridors unverifiable |\n| Mix rationality | 3 | Balanced: buyback ~$100M+/yr, dividend, development, debt paydown |\n| Guidance accuracy | 2 | Record Q2-2026 beat; but Q1-2026 missed prior year (lumpiness is structural) |\n| **Total** | **11/15** | **Good** |\n\nInsider ownership: 0.36% \u2014 no bonus; insider transaction data [Unconfirmed]. **Final 11/15.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\n### 2.5-1/2. R&D & SBC\nR&D not meaningful for this business. SBC was $1M in FY2025 [Actual, filed] \u2014 deducted below.\n\n### 2.5-3. Operating leases\nCapitalised at 7\u00d7: $2M \u00d7 7 \u2248 $14M \u2014 immaterial. [Arithmetic on filed]\n\n### 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC\nAdjusted invested capital = equity $776M + total debt $575M \u2212 cash $117M + leases $14M \u2248 $1,248M [Estimate; vendor debt]. Adjusted NOPAT = $146M \u00d7 (1\u22120.22) \u2248 $114M (+JV income ~$5\u201310M) \u2248 $120M [Estimate]. **Adjusted ROIC \u2248 9.6\u201310.0% vs GAAP ~11.5\u201312.5%** \u2014 the gap (~2%) arises because the massive land base is carried at historical cost; fair-value land would lower the ratio further. This is a modest-return-on-capital business underneath the land uplift.\n\n### 2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett)\nOCF $191M \u2212 maintenance capex ~$20M [Assumption] \u2212 SBC $1M = **~$170M**. Note: development spend (~$90M/yr [Estimate]) is growth investment, not maintenance; on that basis, **distributable owner earnings \u2248 $85\u2013110M/yr**.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINES 3 + 4 \u2014 HYBRID + EXPECTATION BLEND)\n\n### 0-1/0-2. Enterprise classification & engine selection\n**Archetype D \u2014 Product-Platform Hybrid**: the \"product\" is land/communities (165k acres, 25,000-unit residential pipeline); the \"platform\" is the Virtuous Circle (hospitality discovery, club membership, commercial leasing \u2014 recurring monetisation of the installed base).\n\n**Engine selection \u2014 60% Engine 3 (Hybrid: core operating DCF + platform premium) / 40% Engine 4 (Expectation-driven: land optionality).**\n\nTransition check (Option-Led \u2192 Hybrid): Core revenue >50% \u2713 (leasing + hospitality \u2248 48\u201355% of revenue); GM >30% \u2713 (44.1% FY2025); FCF+ for 2 consecutive quarters \u2713. The company is **past the Hybrid threshold but retains a material expectation-driven land component**, justifying the 60/40 blend. Using Engine 1 (pure DCF) alone would understate the land option; using Engine 5 (binary) would be inappropriate. Both alternatives would misprice.\n\n### 3-1. Segment context [Estimate \u2014 pack holds no segment dimensions]\nQ2-2026 mix: Real estate (homesites/commercial) $69.6M (43.8%), Hospitality $74.2M (46.7%), Leasing $15.0M (9.4%), all [Actual, Q2-2026 call]. FY2025 segment split [Unconfirmed] but reconstructed on this mix.\n\n### 3-2. DCF forecast \u2014 Base case (all driver sources labelled)\n\n**Revenue path.** FY2015\u2192FY2025 CAGR 17.3%; 5-yr CAGR 26.2% [Arithmetic on filed]. TTM $548M [Filed]. Base: 2026E $560M (+9.2%) then decelerating 8/7/6/5/4/4/4/4/4% to **$876M by 2035**. Source: company guidance does not extend beyond 2026 [Unconfirmed beyond current year]; deceleration is [Assumption] justified by the finite 25,000-unit homesite pipeline at historical delivery rates (600\u2013900/yr \u2192 30\u201340-year monetisation) and club membership growth of 172/yr (~5%). **The path beyond 2026 is the analyst's assumption, not guidance.**\n\n| Year | Revenue ($M) | Growth | OPM | D&A ($M) | Capex ($M) | NOPAT ($M) | FCFF ($M) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026E | 560 | +9.2% | 27.5% | 50 | 92 | 120 | 78 |\n| 2027E | 605 | +8% | 28.5% | 53 | 88 | 134 | 99 |\n| 2028E | 647 | +7% | 29.5% | 56 | 95 | 149 | 110 |\n| 2029E | 686 | +6% | 30% | 59 | 100 | 161 | 120 |\n| 2030E | 720 | +5% | 30% | 62 | 105 | 168 | 125 |\n| 2031E | 749 | +4% | 30% | 64 | 109 | 175 | 130 |\n| 2032E | 779 | +4% | 30% | 67 | 114 | 182 | 135 |\n| 2033E | 810 | +4% | 30% | 70 | 118 | 190 | 142 |\n| 2034E | 842 | +4% | 30% | 72 | 123 | 197 | 146 |\n| 2035E | 876 | +4% | 30% | 75 | 128 | 205 | 152 |\n\n**Driver sources:**\n- **OPM expansion 27.5%\u219230%**: Q2-2026 segment gross margins were the highest in years (Residential 48%, Hospitality 41%, Commercial 65% [Actual, transcript]); margin expansion is [Estimate] and conservative.\n- **Capex as % of revenue (14\u201316%)**: H1-2026 actual capex $44.7M [Actual, company releases], including two utility corridors beginning 2026, Park Place East (~2,000 units approved), and Ticheli DSAP groundbreaking in 2027 [Actual, transcript]. Full-year 2026 projected ~$92M. This is the **broad capex definition** (Management's definition), not the pack's $4M P,P&E tag. The full-year land/development investing line was not retrievable [Unconfirmed].\n- **\u0394Working capital = $0**: pack does not hold current assets/liabilities for any year [Unconfirmed \u2014 silent zero, disclosed per framework discipline].\n- **Tax rate 22%**: statutory + Florida state blend [Assumption \u2014 pack's pre-tax inputs are flagged broken, do not reconstruct].\n\n### 3-3. WACC derivation\n| Component | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Risk-free (10Y UST) | 4.70% | TradingEconomics 2026-08-21 [Actual] |\n| Equity risk premium | 4.80% | [Assumption] |\n| Beta | 1.25 | Blend of vendor 1.29 [Aggregator] with asset-structure logic: 84% fixed-rate debt reduces equity beta \u2192 1.20\u20131.30 range [Estimate] |\n| Cost of equity | 4.70 + 1.25 \u00d7 4.80 = **10.70%** | [Arithmetic] |\n| Pre-tax cost of debt | 5.20% | [Estimate \u2014 above the 4.7% blended including legacy fixed] |\n| After-tax cost of debt | 5.20 \u00d7 (1\u22120.22) = **4.06%** | [Arithmetic] |\n| Weights (E/V; D/V) | 3,926 / 4,384 = 89.6%; 458 / 4,384 = 10.4% | [Arithmetic] |\n| **WACC** | 0.896\u00d710.70 + 0.104\u00d74.06 = **10.0%** (rounded 9.8\u201310.0%) | Use 10.0% |\n\n*(Using 10.0% rather than 9.8% \u2014 the debt weight is small; sensitivity below.)*\n\n### 3-4. DCF result (Base)\n| Sum of PV, explicit 2026E\u20132035E | ~$765M |\n|---|---|\n| Terminal value = 152\u00d71.03 / (0.10\u22120.03) | $2,237M |\n| PV of terminal @ 10.0% | ~$865M |\n| **EV (DCF)** | **~$1,630M** |\n| Less net debt | $458M |\n| **Equity value** | **~$1,172M \u2192 $20.6/share** |\n\nSensitivity: WACC 9.0% \u2192 ~$27/sh; WACC 11.0% \u2192 ~$16/sh; terminal 2.5%/3.5% \u2192 \u00b1$3. **The pure going-concern DCF is far below the market price.**\n\n### 3-5. Engine 3 platform premium + Engine 4 land optionality \u2014 Sum-of-the-parts\n| Component | Value ($M) | Method / source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Hospitality (FY26E rev ~$280M, GM 41%, EBITDA ~$65M) | 780 | 12\u00d7 EBITDA [Assumption \u2014 club memberships are high-retention recurring] |\n| Leasing/commercial (NOI ~$28M) | 400 | 7.0% cap rate [Assumption] |\n| Residential development (pretax ~$55M) | 550 | 10\u00d7 [Assumption] \u2014 pipeline is the driver |\n| **Undeveloped land (165,000 acres)** | **900\u20131,650** | **$5\u201310k/acre on developable core + $1\u20132k on conservation/timber [Estimate \u2014 key assumption]** |\n| JV investments | 80 | 1.2\u00d7 book of $66.1M [Estimate] |\n| Cash | 117 | [Actual 06-30-26] |\n| Debt (vendor total + leases) | (575) | [Estimate \u2014 vendor scalar] |\n| **SOTP equity** | **$2,250\u20133,000M** | **\u2192 $40\u201353/share** |\n\n### 3-6. Tornado sensitivity \u2014 top 3 variables\n1. **Land value per developable acre** ($5k\u201315k \u2192 \u00b1$12/share) \u2014 *Thesis KPI: acreage sold and $/acre by quarter.*\n2. **2030\u201335 revenue trajectory** ($700M\u20131.2B in 2035 \u2192 \u00b1$10/share) \u2014 *Thesis KPI: homesite deliveries and club member adds.*\n3. **WACC** (9\u201311% \u2192 \u00b1$5/share) \u2014 *Thesis KPI: 10Y UST and JOE's spread.*\n\n### 3-7. Reverse DCF \u2014 what the market is paying\nEV $4.38B vs my explicit-FCFF PV of $765M implies a terminal value of ~$8.7B (\u2248$592M terminal FCFF at 10%/3%). At a 19% FCFF margin, that requires **2035 revenue \u2248 $2.5\u20133.1B** \u2014 4\u20135\u00d7 the 2025 base and ~3\u20133.5\u00d7 my base case. **Verdict: Excessive** \u2014 requires the full developable land bank to be monetised within ~10 years at current spreads, hospitality to triple, and no margin erosion. Per base rates (\u00a71.5), this outcome has low probability.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n1. **Competitive response:** Homebuilders (D.R. Horton, Lennar, Taylor Morrison) are active in the Panhandle and provide constant supply of competing finished lots; a JOE price premium would be competed away over time. Counter-move risk: \u22122.\n2. **Reflexivity:** Positive \u2014 high stock price funds buybacks with equity and signals strength to homebuyers. Negative \u2014 a falling price would not threaten funding (no capital markets dependence) but would slow buyback and dampen buyer confidence. Reflexivity is mild: +1.\n3. **Customer evolution:** Early adopters (second-home investors) \u2192 mainstream primary-home buyers. The $200k\u2013$5M price band is deliberately broad to ride this curve. Risk: late-cycle marginal buyers are rate-sensitive.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIO ENGINE (BAYESIAN)\n\n### 4-1/2. Scenarios (probabilities sum to 100%; base is most probable)\n| Scenario | Prob. | 2035 revenue | Valuation logic | Price |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bear** \u2014 housing recession, 10Y >5.5%, insurance crisis deepens, deliveries <300 homesites/yr | 30% | ~$540M, OPM 24% | SOTP at $3\u20134k/acre land + 0.9\u00d7 book real estate \u2192 **$18** | $18 |\n| **Base** \u2014 continued migration at 2015\u201325 trend, decelerating growth; land $6\u201310k/acre; 50/50 DCF-SOTP | 45% | $876M, OPM 30% | DCF $21 + SOTP $45 weighted \u2192 **$34** | $34 |\n| **Bull** \u2014 NW Florida becomes a sustained job-creation hub (2028 hospital, defense/tech corridor); deliveries accelerate to ~1,500/yr; land $15k/acre | 25% | $1,150\u20131,300M, OPM 32% | SOTP $58\u201362; call it **$58** | $58 |\n\n### 4-3. Bayesian update\nPrior: base rate of land-bank success 30\u201340%. The Q2-2026 print (record $158.9M revenue, +23%; NI $40.5M, +37%) is a positive likelihood \u2014 update Base 40%\u219245%. Offsetting: 10Y UST +44bp YoY (negative evidence) caps the update. Posterior probabilities above.\n\n### 4-4/5. Expected value, CIs, MoS\n- **E[Price] = 0.30\u00d718 + 0.45\u00d734 + 0.25\u00d758 = $35.20.**\n- 50% CI: $27\u201345; 80% CI: $18\u201358.\n- MoS at $68.89 = (35.20\u221268.89)/35.20 = **\u221295.7% \u2192 None.**\n- Expected 5-year return \u2248 (35.2/68.9)^(1/5) \u2212 1 + 0.9% dividend \u2248 **\u221212.2%/yr.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4.5 \u2014 TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\nMarket-implied horizon: reverse DCF shows the price embeds >10 years of near-perfect land monetisation. My horizon: 3\u20135 years. Spread >5 years \u2192 **arbitrage exists on the short side** (mean-reversion expected), but the land-asset floor and active $100M+/yr buyback make the short side executionally risky. Horizon arbitrage conclusion: the *long* side is a poor payoff; the short side has positive expectancy but poor risk-reward in the hands of a forced seller.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 5 \u2014 CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\n| Component | Score | Basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Business quality | 3.5/4 | Wide moat, improving margins, Good balance sheet |\n| Valuation attractiveness | 0.5/4 | Price 2\u00d7 the base IV; MoS deeply negative |\n| Macro alignment | 2/4 | Neutral-to-headwind (10Y 4.7%, rising) |\n| Thesis clarity | 3/3 | KPI-rich: homesite deliveries, members, GM, occupancy |\n| Execution traceability | 2/3 | Long record; lumpiness makes tracking noisy |\n| **Conviction score** | **11/15 \u2014 High** (on the *sell/avoid* side) | |\n| Conviction decay | \u22121 every 6 months without confirmatory evidence | |\n\n**Sizing:** The framework's \"High conviction\" sizing (6\u20138%) applies to longs. Here, the conviction is that the *current price is unattractive*. Long weight: **0%**. No capital should be committed at $68.89.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 5.5 \u2014 BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\n| Metric | Value | Read |\n|---|---|---|\n| Short % float | 3.84%, 974,426 sh, 3.97 DTC, +17.8% MoM | Shorts adding \u2014 but low by absolute standard; no squeeze fuel |\n| Institutional ownership | 88.1% | Index-heavy; ETF flows can add mechanical buying AND selling |\n| Insider ownership | 0.36% | No skin-in-game; [Unconfirmed \u2014 no 6-mo insider data] |\n| Options positioning | Not retrieved | [Unconfirmed] |\n| 52-week range | $46.37\u201373.54; price near high | Positive momentum; risk of momentum unwind |\n| Dividend yield | 0.94% | Income investors irrelevant to the bid |\n| **Crowding risk score** | **8/15 \u2014 Moderate** | Position-size adjustment: \u22120% from long sizing (already 0%) |\n\nSentiment: post-record-Q2 euphoria is embedded in a 32\u00d7 trailing multiple \u2014 a **contrarian warning signal**, not a buy signal.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 6 \u2014 PORTFOLIO FIT\n\nJOE is a leveraged play on (a) Florida Panhandle migration, (b) US housing affordability, (c) discretionary tourism/hospitality, and (d) long-duration land values. It behaves like a mid-cap homebuilder with a wider volatility envelope (beta 1.29 [Aggregator]). In a portfolio context: it offers **no** defensive properties, low dividend, high energy on the downside in a rate shock, and high correlation with Sun Belt banks (homebuilders, regional banks, other Florida REITs).\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 6.5 \u2014 KELLY SIZING\n\nPayouts to a LONG from $68.89: Bear $18 (\u221274%), Base $34 (\u221251%), Bull $58 (\u221216%). E[Payoff] = 0.30\u00d7(\u22120.74) + 0.45\u00d7(\u22120.51) + 0.25\u00d7(\u22120.16) = \u22120.222 \u2212 0.229 \u2212 0.040 = **\u221249% expected return**.\n- **Full-Kelly long weight: \u2212NN (negative) \u2192 0%.**\n- Short-side Kelly from $68.89 to E[$35.20] \u2248 +49% gross with fat-tail land risk: raw Kelly ~10\u201320% of risk budget \u2192 **rejected** due to (1) $100M+/yr company buyback creating a persistent bid, (2) low-trust land comps, (3) rising short crowding (+17.8% MoM). **Final: 0% both directions.**\n- Sizing gap vs \u00a75: 0% vs 0% \u2014 no re-analysis required.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 7 \u2014 RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\n### Catalysts (what could power the stock)\n1. 2028 academic health centre/teaching hospital opening [Actual, transcript]\n2. Lake Powell/West Laird/Pigeon Creek/West Bay Creek utility corridors breaking ground [Actual, transcript]\n3. Park Place East (~2,000 units) entitlements [Actual]\n4. Buyback acceleration: $32.7M in Q2-26 alone [Actual]\n5. A Fed pivot toward easing \u2192 mortgage-rate relief, multiple expansion\n\n### Risks (what could break it)\n1. **10Y >5.5%** \u2014 housing affordability collapse in the entry-level $200\u2013400k band\n2. **Florida property-insurance crisis worsening** \u2014 direct cost to homeowners and commercial tenants\n3. Development delays on the two utility corridors (cost overruns, permitting)\n4. Homesite delivery slippage (Q1-2026's \u221220% NI was a warning shot)\n5. Index/ETF flow reversal if the stock is dropped from momentum screens\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 7.5 \u2014 CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\n- Common bear trigger with the rest of a growth/value portfolio: **rising real yields**. JOE's duration is long (land, memberships); its drawdown correlation in 2022-style stress would approach 0.8\u20130.9 with homebuilders and Sun Belt REITs.\n- **Tail correlation:** in 2008-style stress, land values fell 40\u201360% in Florida; JOE's equity would retest the NAV floor ($10\u201318), a \u221274% to \u221282% CVaR from $68.89 in the worst decile.\n- Portfolio rule: no single position with 10%+ CVaR overlap should exceed 1\u20132% weight. **JOE should not be held in a rate-sensitive portfolio at this price.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 8 \u2014 MONITORING RULES\n\n### Thesis integrity thresholds (for a would-be long at a lower price)\n| KPI | Trigger | Response |\n|---|---|---|\n| Quarterly homesite deliveries | <300 twice in a row | Thesis broken \u2014 exit/reassess |\n| Watersound Club net adds | <100/qtr | Platform thesis weakening |\n| Residential gross margin | <40% | Pricing power eroding |\n| Commercial occupancy | <90% | Leasing value impaired |\n| 10Y UST | >5.5% sustained | Structural headwind \u2014 cut |\n| Price | \u2264$35 | Re-run full analysis; only entry zone |\n\n### Pre-mortem: \"If JOE fails by 2031, why?\"\n1. **Housing recession 2027\u201328** \u2014 deliveries halve; early-warning: 3 consecutive quarters of declining signed contracts.\n2. **Hospitality saturation** \u2014 club membership stalls below 5,000; early-warning: member adds <100 for 4 quarters.\n3. **Entitlement/insurance rupture** \u2014 one of the DSAPs is blocked or insurance makes new-home economics negative; early-warning: rising unsold finished-home inventory in the region.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 9 \u2014 RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\n### 9-1. Short thesis (5\u20137 bullets, same data)\n1. At $68.89 the market capitalises 165,000 acres at ~$15k/acre \u2014 a price paid for *developable* Panhandle land, despite material conservation/wetland/timber acreage inside the 165k. The market is charging top dollar for every acre.\n2. Reverse DCF demands 2035E revenue of $2.5\u20133.1B \u2014 a 4\u20135\u00d7 expansion that no company-specific data (25,000 lots at ~600\u2013900/yr delivery, club adds of 172/yr) supports.\n3. 32\u00d7 trailing P/E on a lumpy developer with a 3.1% earnings yield vs 4.7% risk-free \u2014 negative carry against the risk-free asset.\n4. Q1-2026 (\u221220% YoY NI) demonstrates the \"seeding and harvesting\" cyclicality that makes 32\u00d7 multiple unjustifiable.\n5. Florida insurance costs are a structural, compounding headwind on the very asset class JOE sells.\n6. Institutional ownership 88% + rising short interest + near-high price = poor marginal-buyer physics.\n7. Insider ownership 0.36% \u2014 management's wealth is not aligned with the equity at this margin.\n\n### 9-2. Logic combat \u2014 Bull rebuttal\n(1) Land is worth more than my comps; (2) scarcity value of 165k acres in the fastest-migrating US region; (3) the Virtuous Circle is real and quantifiable; (4) $100M+/yr buyback is a self-funding floor; (5) 84% fixed at 4.7% is a crisis shield; (6) the 2028 hospital + utility corridors are credible unlockers; (7) the balance sheet can outlast any downturn.\n\n**Score: Short logic 7/10; Long logic 6/10.** Gap = 1 < 2 \u2192 **conviction on the negative view steps down 0.5** \u2014 meaning: do not short, do not be arrogant about the target, keep the wide 80% CI.\n\n### 9-3. Adversarial documents\nThe research brief cites an \"8-K dated 2026-08-06\" with unspecified material/legal content. **EDGAR's JOE filing list shows no 8-K on that date**; the most recent is 2026-07-29 (Q2 earnings). The brief's citation traces to May 4, 2026, item 8.01 disclosure (small format). [Unconfirmed] \u2014 resolved in the company's favour unless a new filing appears. No Hindenburg-grade adversary has a published report on JOE [Unconfirmed].\n\n### 9-4. Killed arguments (and the conditions that would revive them)\n- *\"JOE is a short because of the August 6, 2026 8-K.\"* **Killed** \u2014 filing could not be found; revive only if a genuine 8-K with litigation/regulatory content is filed.\n- *\"The balance sheet is a fortress.\"* **Partially killed** \u2014 vendor debt $573M vs my earlier $392M LT-only reading; net debt/EBITDA 2.4\u00d7 is manageable, not fortress.\n- *\"The market is right because land value is unknowable from the book.\"* **Revived-if** \u2014 comparable sales data show $15k+/acre on large tracts to be standard; then the bull case approaches $58+ and my Base is too low. This is the single biggest risk to my conclusion and is explicitly carried in the 25% bull probability.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 10 \u2014 AUDIT RESULTS (37-point summary)\n\n| Category | Result | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| A. Methodology | **Pass** | Engines 3+4 blended; no DCF-only conclusion; SOTP + reverse DCF + multiples as cross-checks |\n| B. Valuation | **Pass w/ caveats** | WACC 10.0% explicit; terminal 3% \u2264 nominal GDP; \u0394WC assumed 0 [Unconfirmed \u2014 pack cannot compute]; capex treated on broad, Management-consistent definition |\n| C. Bias | **Pass** | Base case anchored within base rates; not anchored on the record-Q2 narrative; explicit overconfidence penalty applied (Red Team) |\n| D. Consistency | **Pass** | T0 = 2026-08-24; T-1 = FY2025 filed; Tpf = Q2-2026 call; no mixing |\n| E. Narrative | **Pass** | \"Virtuous Circle\" quantified via members, deliveries, GM \u2014 no unquantified platform premium |\n| F. Red Team | **Pass** | Full adversarial pass in \u00a79; gap score 1 \u2192 downgraded conviction |\n| G. Data integrity | **Pass w/ flags** | FY2014\u21922015 revenue basis change not used in trends; capex tag disagreement disclosed; vendor total debt vs filed LT debt conflict disclosed; Aug-6 8-K unverifiable |\n\n**Pass on all points.** One recalculation was triggered (reverse DCF) and performed; conclusion after recalculation: sustained.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 11 \u2014 DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n\n- **Date / T0:** 2026-08-24; JOE @ $68.89; mcap $3.926B; EV ~$4.38B.\n- **Intrinsic value:** Base $34; 80% CI $18\u201358; Expected value $35.20.\n- **Key assumptions logged:** WACC 10.0% (10Y 4.7%, ERP 4.8%, beta 1.25, COD 5.2%); terminal g 3.0%; 2035E revenue $876M; capex 14\u201316% of revenue on broad definition; land $5\u201310k/acre on developable core.\n- **Red Team concerns logged:** (1) land comps could be conservative \u2014 [Estimate]; (2) market may be pricing a genuine NW-Florida super-cycle (hospital 2028, corridors); (3) buyback is a persistent floor. Net: no short; no long.\n- **Consensus position:** Company delivered record Q2; market extrapolated it; I did not.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 12 \u2014 FINAL EXECUTION OPINION\n\n| Decision Item | Recommendation |\n|---|---|\n| **Action** | **SELL if held; DO NOT INITIATE if not held.** No long position valuation support at $68.89. |\n| **Conviction** | High (11/15) on the *negative valuation view*; tempered by Red Team gap \u2014 do not express via shorting. |\n| **Weight** | **0%** long; **0%** short. If held, exit into any strength toward $70\u201373 (52-wk high zone) in a planned, split tranche (1/3 now, 1/3 at $70+, 1/3 within 60 days) to reduce market-timing risk. |\n| **Re-entry zone** | **\u2264$35** \u2014 the 50% CI lower edge \u2014 with confirmation that homesite deliveries are \u2265400/qtr and 10Y \u22645.0%. |\n| **Monitoring if held** | Execute \u00a78 thresholds; revisit on: 3 consecutive quarters of delivery growth, proof of $15k+/acre land comps, or 10Y break below 4.0%. |\n| **Key risk to this opinion** | Land-value understatement (Bull $58). If comparable large-tract NW-Florida sales consistently print >$15k/acre, the Base case moves toward $45\u201350 and the SELL is wrong. Probability-weighted, this is inside the 80% CI and acceptable. |\n\n**Final statement:** The St. Joe Company is a wide-moat, well-managed, but *luxuriously priced* land developer. Underneath the record 2026 headlines, the cash-flow math supports ~$21\u201334/share; the market is paying $68.89 for a decade of near-perfect land monetisation. The probability-weighted payoff at T0 is **negative ~49%**, and the margin of safety is **\u221296%**. The price embeds the bull case; the prudent investor demands compensation for the deep cyclical and insurance risks of Northwest Florida. **Sell into strength; watch from the sidelines at a 50% lower price.**\n\n---\n\n*End of report \u2014 RS2 v2.0. All estimates tagged. Data gaps marked [Unconfirmed] as required.*"}, {"sample": 2, "iv": 62.5, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 379, "report": "```\nSECTION 0. ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nT0 Pri