LE (one sentence): JOE is a high-quality, wide-moat land-and-recurring-\nrevenue platform, but at $68.89 the market is paying slightly above the\nprobability-weighted expected value of $65.00, with a hawkish Fed, Florida home\nprices already rolling over, and the largest holder trimming \u2014 so the risk/reward\nis unattractive for new money today.\n\nFINAL RATING: HOLD\n```"}, {"sample": 3, "iv": 59.0, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 325, "report": "```text\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 RS2 v2.0 INTEGRATED INVESTMENT ANALYSIS \u2014 THE ST. JOE COMPANY (NYSE: JOE)\n\u25a0 T0: 2026-08-24 | T-1: FY2025 (2025-12-31) | Tpf: Q2 2026 (2026-06-30)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nSECTION 0. ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTicker: JOE | Sector: Real Estate | Industry: Real Estate-Diversified\nPrice (T0): $68.89 [Aggregator, 2026-08-24] | Shares: 56,991,700 [Aggregator]\nMarket Cap: $3.926B [Arithmetic: price \u00d7 shares]\nNet Debt (my definition): $0.264B [Arithmetic: LT debt $0.391B + finance lease $0.001B + operating lease $0.002B \u2212 cash $0.130B, FY2025; components [Filed]]\nEnterprise Value: ~$4.19B [Arithmetic]\n52-Week Range: $46.37\u2013$73.54 [Aggregator]\nTrailing P/E: 32.1 | P/B: 5.10 | EV/S (FY26E): ~7.2x [Arithmetic on [Estimate]]\nDividend Yield: 0.94% [Aggregator]\n\nPrimary Conclusion: HOLD \u2014 NO NEW POSITION AT $68.89.\nProbability-weighted intrinsic value \u2248 $59/share (50% CI: $48\u2013$72; 80% CI: $38\u2013$88).\nMargin of Safety \u2248 \u221217% (negative). The market is paying for the bull case today\nwhile macro conditions (Fed hiking, elevated 10Y at ~4.7%) are a headwind for a\nrate-sensitive land developer. Long-term quality is real \u2014 wide moat, scarce land,\nexpanding pipeline \u2014 but entry at $68.89 offers an expected 3-year annualized total\nreturn of roughly 5\u20136%, below the ~10% cost of equity. Wait for $55\u201358 or a\ndemonstrable rate reversal before initiating/adding.\n\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT  [Layered: 1-1 \u2192 1-2 \u2192 1-3 \u2192 1-4]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 1-1. Macro Variable Check\n- 10Y Treasury: 4.71\u20134.74% as of 2026-08-21, up 25\u201330bp over the FOMC intermeeting period, +44bp YoY. [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics; FRED DGS10]\n- Real rates: Elevated and RISING; the Fed minutes attribute the nominal yield rise to real-rate increases. [Actual \u2014 FOMC Minutes 2026-07-29]\n- Dollar (DXY): Edged up; dollar continued to appreciate on resilient US growth. [Actual \u2014 FOMC Minutes]\n- Fed stance: TIGHTENING bias. IORB 3.65% effective 2026-07-30; market fully prices +25bp by September 2026 and another by Q1 2027. The median Desk survey respondent expects no further change in 2027 and a cut only in early 2028. [Actual \u2014 Federal Reserve]\n- Inflation: Total PCE 3.7% (June est.), core PCE 3.3%; 12-month total PCE was 4.1% in May. Tariff effects, Middle East oil shock, and AI-buildout demand are the stated drivers. [Actual \u2014 FOMC Minutes]\n- Global liquidity: Reserves ample; repo briefly soft but recovered. Foreign central banks mostly on hold; US policy rate path higher than AFEs. [Actual \u2014 FOMC Minutes]\n- Economic cycle: LATE EXPANSION. Real GDP slowed in Q2 but private domestic purchases firmed; unemployment 4.2%; payroll growth slowed in June but H1 pace above 2025. [Actual \u2014 FOMC Minutes]\n- Credit spreads: IG stable; private-credit redemption requests at BDCs rose in Q2 \u2014 early stress indicator. [Actual \u2014 FOMC Minutes]\n\nStep 1-2. Regime Classification\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 10% \u2014 excluded by the actual rate path.\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): 50% \u2014 current reality; AI capex + consumer spending + tariff/oil inflation.\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 25% \u2014 Middle East oil shock + tariff persistence + Fed hiking into slowing GDP is the live risk.\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 15% \u2014 typical late-cycle hiking path ends in a downturn; the Fed may be forced to cut fast if credit stress spreads.\nSum = 100%. [Assumption \u2014 my probability distribution based on [Actual] FOMC/FRED inputs]\n\nStep 1-3. Macro Sensitivity (5 axes, -3 to +3)\n- Interest-rate sensitivity: \u22123 (mortgage rates directly drive residential lot demand; 84% of debt fixed, but new development financing is exposed)\n- Dollar sensitivity: +1 (strong dollar attracts foreign visitors to Florida; immaterial direct revenue exposure)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: \u22122 (tightening \u2192 buyer credit for second homes and lot loans)\n- Economic-cycle sensitivity: \u22122 (land sales are discretionary, lumpy; hospitality is travel-cyclical)\n- Commodity sensitivity: \u22121 (oil affects hospitality energy and construction costs; long-run land value benefits from inflation)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment Judgment\nRegime-weighted expected macro impact \u2248 \u22121.3 on the \u22123/+3 scale \u2192 HEADWIND.\nConviction \u22120.5 steps; split-entry only; size cap reduced. Macro does not change intrinsic\nvalue, only position sizing and entry timing.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN  [Layered: 1.5-1 \u2192 1.5-2 \u2192 1.5-3]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 1.5-1. Base Rates (reference set from framework; consistent with US real-estate developer history)\n- 5-year survival for a well-capitalized, debt-light land owner (170k acres owned, ~1.4x net debt/EBITDA): >90%.\n- Probability that a land developer sustains ROIC > WACC over a full cycle: ~40\u201350% (many overpay for land or time the cycle wrong).\n- Probability of a \u226540% drawdown in residential land-sales revenue when mortgage rates spike/housing turns: ~30\u201340% (JOE's own 2015 vs 2014 pattern, even after adjusting for basis change, shows how far land revenue can fall).\n- Probability that a hospitality\u2192membership\u2192homebuyer \"ecosystem\" transition becomes a durable platform: ~20\u201330%.\n- Probability that a 170k-acre land bank realizes full mark-to-market NAV within a decade: LOW (<30%); land monetization is multi-decade by nature.\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation of This Analysis from Base Rate\nMy bull/base scenario assumes continued 5\u201310% consolidated revenue growth through 2035 and expanding margins. That is roughly 1.5x the base rate for a cyclical land developer. Justifications:\n1. Structurally growing recurring base: Hospitality + Leasing = 56% of Q2-2026 revenue ($89.2M of $158.8M) [Actual \u2014 Fool transcript], with Watersound Club membership compounding (+172 YoY to 3,723) [Actual].\n2. Regulatory moat: Bay-Walton Sector Plan covering 110,500 acres; DSAP approvals take years and represent a huge competitor barrier. 25,000-unit residential pipeline approved/entitled/platted, with 2,000+ platted/under development [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026 transcript].\n3. Record achieved results: Q2-2026 revenue +23% YoY to $158.8M; NI +37% to $40.5M; residential GM 48%; hospitality GM 41%; leasing/commercial GM 65% [Actual \u2014 transcript]. This is evidence of execution, not just narrative.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check\nSuccess probability embedded in my base case (~65% for the \"base+bull\" scenarios) is just above 1.5x the ~40\u201350% base rate for the archetype. Not a red flag (>2.0x), but the 50% scenario is weighted to the lower end of growth to avoid over-optimism. Flag: elevated, monitor the residential sales cadence and true-up collections quarterly.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY  [Layered: 2-1 \u2192 2-5]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding\nThe St. Joe Company is a diversified real estate developer and operator owning ~170,000 acres of land in Bay and Walton Counties, Northwest Florida. It sells residential homesites and commercial ground (with contractual \"true-ups\" tied to homebuilder orders) while operating a growing hospitality platform \u2014 Watersound Club memberships, hotels, golf, beach clubs \u2014 and a commercial leasing portfolio of 1.196M sq ft at 95% occupancy. The \"Virtuous Circle of Value Creation\" drives hospitality guests into homebuyers and tenants, monetizing a scarce, low-cost land base across multiple cycles.\nUnderstandability: MEDIUM \u2014 the core is simple, but lot-sale timing, true-up mechanics, and JV structures add complexity.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Assessment + Dynamics\nIntangible assets (brand): 2/3 \u2014 WaterColor/Watersound/\"30A\" association; regional, not national.\nSwitching costs: 1/3 \u2014 low for individual homebuyers; moderate for club members and commercial tenants.\nNetwork effects: 1/3 \u2014 density and community ecosystem add modest pull; not a two-sided tech network.\nCost advantage: 3/3 \u2014 170k acres of low-basis land in a land-constrained, high-growth corridor is a structural cost advantage that cannot be replicated at market prices.\nEconomies of scale: 2/3 \u2014 utility infrastructure, DSAP planning, and hospital/health campus create scale benefits.\nRegulatory barriers: 3/3 \u2014 DSAP/Sector Plan approvals take 5\u201310+ years; JOE's 110,500-acre Sector Plan creates an unmatched permitting moat.\nData moat: 0/3 \u2014 member data exists but is not monetized.\nMOAT SCORE: 12/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT. Direction: WIDENING \u2014 based on 25,000-unit pipeline expansion, two new utility corridors starting 2026, Park Place East (~2,000 units) approved, Ticheli DSAP break-ground next year, and gross-margin expansion across all segments. [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026 transcript]\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification\nCYCLICAL (with growing Stalwart component). The residential land segment prints lumpy, cycle-driven results; hospitality/leasing provides a growing, recurring anchor. Checkpoints: watch lot-sales velocity, true-up cash collections, membership growth, occupancy, and regional housing demand \u2014 all currently positive.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard (all [Filed] unless noted)\n- Revenue CAGR (FY2021\u2013FY2025): (0.513/0.267)^(1/4) \u2212 1 = 17.7%; 5-yr trailing CAGR: +26.2% [Arithmetic]\n- Latest operating margin: 28.5% (TTM); 12-year median: 24.5% [Arithmetic]\n- EPS/NI growth: FY2022\u2192FY2025 NI CAGR 17.8% [Arithmetic]\n- ROE (TTM): 0.123/0.776 = 15.9%; ROIC \u2248 10.5% [Estimate \u2014 net income / (debt + equity \u2212 cash)]\n- FCF margin (my definition incl. full investment): ~25% [Estimate]\n- Net debt/EBITDA: 0.264/(0.146+0.047) = 1.4x [Estimate]\n- Interest coverage: >5x [Estimate]\nJudgment: GOOD. Adequate-to-strong, with the caveat that reported capex is far below the true capital investment because land/community development is capitalized on the balance sheet.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard\n1. Buyback efficiency: 3/3 \u2014 $32.7M repurchased in Q2 2026 (499,700 shares \u2248 $65.4 avg); shares outstanding at ~56.9M, the LOWEST in ~30 years; buyback executed below the 52-week high. [Actual \u2014 transcript]\n2. M&A track record: 2/3 \u2014 Latitude Margaritaville Watersound JV scaling (+25% net contracts); no major impairments this cycle; Watercrest JV sold at a gain-driven nonstrategic exit. [Actual]\n3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 $24.0M Q2 capex into utility corridors and community development; long-gestation, so incremental ROIC unproven until lots sell. [Estimate]\n4. Rationality of mix: 3/3 \u2014 dividends ($0.16/q), buybacks, development capex, and nonstrategic dispositions balanced coherently.\n5. Guidance accuracy: 2/3 \u2014 company does not give multi-year formal guidance; management flags residential lumpiness; record Q2 beats prior-year handsomely. [Actual]\nTotal: 12/15. Insider ownership 0.36% [Aggregator] \u2014 no +1 bonus; no insider-buy signal; no large insider sell in pack \u2192 no adjustment.\nInsider-adjusted score: 12/15 \u2192 GOOD.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 2.5-1/2/3/4. R&D: N/A. SBC: $0.001B FY2024/25 [Filed] \u2014 immaterial; treat as full expense. Operating leases: $0.002B \u2014 immaterial; skip capitalization. Brand/data: brand embedded in land pricing; no reliable separate value [Unconfirmed].\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC \u2248 GAAP ROIC \u2248 10.5% [Estimate] \u2014 the gap is immaterial because the asset base is already largely on the balance sheet (unlike R&D/software companies).\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (FY2025, Buffett-style):\nNI $0.116B + D&A $0.047B \u2212 maintenance capex (est. $15M; hospitality assets/roads/utilities upkeep [Assumption]) \u2212 \u0394WC ~$0M \u2248 $0.145B. [Estimate]\nTTM (through 2026-06-30): NI $0.123B + D&A (~0.050) \u2212 maint. (~0.015) \u2248 $0.155B. [Estimate]\nCRITICAL: The pack's FY2025 FCF of $0.187B uses PP&E-only capex ($0.004B) and is over-stated. Q2-2026 actual capex was $24M [Actual \u2014 transcript]; true annual investment is $80\u2013120M, not $4M. All cash-flow estimates in this report use the corrected investment line.\n\nSECTION 3. VALUATION  [Blended Engine: Engine 2 (Cycle-Adjusted) 50% + Engine 3 (Hybrid) 50%]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 0-1/0-2 Archetype & Engine Selection\nArchetype: C \u2014 CYCLICAL, in transition toward a D/Hybrid platform model. Recurring revenue\n(Hospitality + Leasing) reached 56% of Q2-2026 revenue, and the \"Virtuous Circle\" behaves\nlike a platform feedback loop. However, the dominant profit driver remains land sales.\nTransition status: <50% of the \"Hybrid \u2192 Quality Compounder\" criteria (ROIC > WACC for 3\nconsecutive years: 2023 ROIC \u2248 6.8% failed; 2024 \u2248 6.2% failed; 2025 \u2248 10.5% passed) \u2192 not\nyet a Quality Compounder.\nEngine: Weighted blend \u2014 Engine 2 (Cycle-Adjusted) 50% + Engine 3 (Hybrid: Core + Platform\nPremium) 50%. A pure Engine 2 would ignore the growing recurring asset base; a pure Engine\n3 would over-weight the platform premium that is still being built. Errors if I chose a\nsingle engine: Engine 1 alone would mechanically extrapolate peak earnings; Engine 4 would\noverstate optionality value for a company with real cash flows.\n\nENGINE 2 \u2014 CYCLE-ADJUSTED (50% weight)\nMid-cycle revenue estimate: $500M \u2014 between FY26E ($585M [Estimate]) and a trough scenario\n(~$400M in a housing downturn, when residential land sales contract 40\u201350% and hospitality\nsoftens 10\u201315%). [Assumption \u2014 based on 2015\u20132016 trough behavior vs 2023\u20132026 peak]\nMid-cycle operating margin: 26% (average of peak 28.5% and trough ~18%). [Assumption]\nMid-cycle net income: $500M \u00d7 26% \u00d7 (1\u22120.21) \u2248 $103M. [Estimate]\nNormalized EPS: $103M / 56.93M = $1.81. [Estimate]\nFair multiple for a cyclical land developer with a growing recurring base: 17x\n(mid-cycle, quality-adjusted; pure cyclicals get 12\u201314x, quality compounders 22x+).\nValue per Engine 2: $1.81 \u00d7 17 = $30.8. Hmm \u2014 this ignores land NAV; see NAV layer below.\nNAV cross-check: 170k acres; land value [Unconfirmed \u2014 no reliable external appraisal found].\nMy range [Assumption]: $8k/acre for remote/unentitled, $25\u201350k+/acre for entitled/30A-adjacent\nland; blended $12k\u2013$20k/acre \u2192 land \u2248 $2.0\u2013$3.4B. Adding hospitality/leasing/recurring\nbusinesses at ~10x segment EBIT \u2248 $0.9B, less net debt $0.264B \u2192 equity \u2248 $2.7\u20134.0B \u2192\n$47\u201370/share. Central NAV estimate: $58/share. [Estimate]\nEngine 2 conclusion: 0.5 \u00d7 ($30.8 + $58)/2 \u2248 $22.2/share (including both earnings-multiple\nand NAV perspectives).\n\nENGINE 3 \u2014 HYBRID (CORE + PLATFORM PREMIUM) (50% weight)\nSegment revenue split FY26E [Estimate based on Q2-2026 actuals [Actual]]:\n- Real Estate (land/homesite sales + true-ups): ~$260M (45%)\n- Hospitality (club, hotels, golf): ~$240M (41%)\n- Leasing (office/retail/commercial): ~$80M (14%)\nTotal FY26E: $580\u2013590M. [Estimate]\nRecurring segment (Hospitality + Leasing) EBIT estimate: revenue ~$320M \u00d7 ~30% EBIT margin\n\u2248 $95M [Assumption]. Apply 12x recurring-platform multiple (membership/leasing businesses\ntrade 12\u201316x) \u2192 $1.14B. [Assumption]\nDevelopment segment EBIT estimate: revenue ~$260M \u00d7 ~25% \u2248 $65M [Assumption]. Apply 8x\ncyclical developer multiple \u2192 $0.52B. [Assumption]\nLand residual value (land not yet in current development): 120k acres \u00d7 $4k\u20136k avg \u2192\n$0.5\u20130.7B. [Assumption]\nSum of parts EV: 1.14 + 0.52 + 0.60 = $2.26B; minus net debt $0.264B \u2192 equity \u2248 $2.0B \u2192\n$35/share. This is a conservative SOTP; it materially UNDERSTATES the value of the\n25,000-unit pipeline beyond the current year, because future lot sales are only partially\nreflected in \"current-year development EBIT.\" Adjusting for pipeline PV:\nPipeline PV: 25,000 units \u00d7 avg PV net margin $40k (after development cost, discounted) =\n$1.0B [Assumption]. Add pipeline PV (partially overlapping with land residual) \u2192 equity \u2248\n$2.5B \u2192 $44/share.\nEngine 3 conclusion: $44/share.\n\nBLENDED IV: 0.5 \u00d7 $44 (Engine 3) + 0.5 \u00d7 $58 (Engine 2-NAV-centric) = $51/share.\nWait \u2014 Engine 2 blend was 0.5\u00d7($30.8+$58)/2 = $22.2 \u2014 that's wrong arithmetic. Let me\ncorrect cleanly:\nEngine 2 result = midpoint of (earnings-multiple value $30.8) and (NAV value $58) = $44.4.\nEngine 3 result = $44.0.\nBlended = 0.5 \u00d7 $44.4 + 0.5 \u00d7 $44.0 = $44.2/share. [Estimate/Assumption-heavy]\n\nIndependent DCF CROSS-CHECK (not the primary engine):\nDiscount-rate derivation:\n- rf = 4.71% [Actual \u2014 FRED]\n- ERP = 4.8% [Assumption; consistent with ~4\u20135% long-run US equity premium]\n- Beta = 1.15 [Assumption \u2014 vendor reports 1.29 [Aggregator] but I fundamentally discount\n  a land-heavy, low-debt asset; 1.15 is my judgment]\n- Cost of equity = 4.71% + 1.15 \u00d7 4.8% = 10.2%\n- After-tax cost of debt = 4.7% \u00d7 (1\u22120.21) = 3.7% [Actual interest rate \u2014 transcript; 21% tax assumption]\n- Debt weight = 264/(3,926+264) = 6.3%\n- WACC = 0.937 \u00d7 10.2% + 0.063 \u00d7 3.7% = 9.8% \u2192 use 10.0% [Assumption]\nExplicit forecast (base case; all [Assumption] after FY2026):\n| Year | Revenue | OPM | OI | NI (\u2248OI\u00d70.79) | OCF (=1.6\u00d7NI) | Total Invest | FCF |\n|------|---------|-----|-----|-----------------|---------------|-------------|-----|\n| FY26E| 0.585 | 28.5% | 0.167 | 0.132 | 0.211 | 0.085 | 0.126 |\n| FY27E| 0.655 | 29.0% | 0.190 | 0.150 | 0.240 | 0.095 | 0.145 |\n| FY28E| 0.720 | 29.5% | 0.212 | 0.168 | 0.269 | 0.100 | 0.169 |\n| FY29E| 0.778 | 30.0% | 0.233 | 0.184 | 0.294 | 0.105 | 0.189 |\n| FY30E| 0.832 | 30.5% | 0.254 | 0.201 | 0.322 | 0.110 | 0.212 |\n| FY31E| 0.882 | 31.0% | 0.273 | 0.216 | 0.346 | 0.115 | 0.231 |\n| FY32E| 0.926 | 31.0% | 0.287 | 0.227 | 0.363 | 0.118 | 0.245 |\n| FY33E| 0.963 | 31.0% | 0.299 | 0.236 | 0.378 | 0.120 | 0.258 |\n| FY34E| 0.992 | 31.0% | 0.308 | 0.243 | 0.389 | 0.122 | 0.267 |\n| FY35E| 1.012 | 31.0% | 0.314 | 0.248 | 0.397 | 0.124 | 0.273 |\nRevenue growth after FY2026: sourced from my own assumptions \u2014 model reaches ~$1B in 10\nyears, i.e., +12%\u2192+3% declining; the company provides no multi-year guidance beyond the\ncurrent-year DSAP pipeline commentary [Unconfirmed for later years]. Capex/investment line\nsourced from Q2-2026 actual $24M/quarter [Actual \u2014 transcript], annualized to ~$96M and\nmodulated for growth. OCF/NI ratio of 1.6x is historical arithmetic on FY2022\u2013FY2025\n(0.048/0.071=0.68? \u2014 no; let me be careful: FY2025 OCF/NI = 191/116 = 1.65; FY2024 = 108/74\n= 1.46; FY2023 = 104/78 = 1.33. Use 1.5x average.) [Arithmetic]\nTerminal value = $273M \u00d7 1.03 / (0.10 \u2212 0.03) = $4.02B. PV factor 10yr @10% = 0.386 \u2192\nPV TV = $1.55B.\nPV of explicit FCF \u2248 $1.22B.\nEV \u2248 $2.77B; + cash $0.13B \u2212 debt $0.394B \u2192 equity \u2248 $2.51B \u2192 $44/share.\nDCF Conclusion: $44/share.\n\n3-X. TORNADO SENSITIVITY (top-3 variables)\n1. WACC (\u00b11%): IV $44 \u2192 $36 (11%) / $56 (8.9%)\n2. Terminal land/pipeline growth rate (\u00b11%): IV $44 \u2192 $38 / $52\n3. Residential GM at cycle peak (\u00b15pp): IV $44 \u2192 $41 / $47\nThese become the Thesis KPIs: WACC (10Y + Fed path), true-up/lot-sales margin, and\npipeline conversion.\n\nFINAL VALUATION ASSEMBLY\n- Blended Engine: $44.2\n- DCF cross-check: $44.0\n- NAV central: $58.0\n- Weight: 40% blended/engine, 20% DCF, 40% NAV (asset-heavy real estate \u2192 NAV deserves the largest weight)\nIV BASE = 0.4\u00d744.2 + 0.2\u00d744.0 + 0.4\u00d758.0 = 17.7 + 8.8 + 23.2 = $49.7 \u2248 $50/share.\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3.5-1. Competitive response: If JOE's residential pipeline executes, other FL panhandle\ndevelopers (e.g., larger national homebuilders) may accelerate their own entitlements; but\nthe DSAP regulatory moat limits supply, so competition is constrained. Net -1 (mild).\n3.5-2. Reflexivity: POSITIVE \u2014